Now a few suggestions from Yours Truly to the Nethack Gods:

1) Regarding the polymorphing controversy: Why not have the wand only
polymorph the "top" object on a pile.  The most objects you could
polymorph in one shot would then be the number you could line up -
subject to the distance limitations of the spell.  This would probably
limit the "feedback" effects now possible with magic lamps, etc.
without totally defeating the polymorph effect.  On this same subject,
I am now totally in agreement with Dave Hairston: Named weapons should
never be created by polymorph.

2) How about a "scroll of blessing".  Blesses an object of your choice,
or if the scroll itself is blessed, a 1/2 possibility of additional
objects.  If the scroll is cursed then it is a scroll of cursing (of course).
A scroll of blessing may optionally bless the square that the player is
on.  This has no effect unless there is an engraving there, whereupon
the engraving becomes permanent.  Now - If you please your God sufficiently,
you will be told the magic letters (much in the way you get told the tune
for the castle gate).  If you engrave the magic letters, then bless the
spot with a scroll of blessing, the spot will be turned into an altar of
your alignment.

3) If you toss gold into a fountain, you should have a small chance of
getting a wish.  You should have a much greater chance of losing the gold
in question.  There probably should be a minimum "ante" for this maneuver.
Other possibilities should include the fountain drying up, summoning a
leprechaun, or getting a rumor from a voice in the fountain.

4) Speaking of fountains:  When I try to dig a pit, I should have a small
chance of hitting water and making a fountain.  The dungeon locations where
that would occur should be preset when the level is built.  Of course these
locations could be determined if I wield a "dowsing rod".  A dowsing rod
should have finite charges, should work only when wielded, and will waste
a charge if I am dumb enough to walk over a fountain or sink while wielding
it.

5) With all this Tolkien stuff in the game, if we have Orcrist we really
ought to have Glamdring.  Also, didn't Aragorn have a sword (Nardil or
something like that..) that was broken and re-forged?  Should be some
possibilities there.  Seems to me that lembas wafers should be a bit
more potent too, with an extra kicker if the player is an elf. 

6) A nice (but rare) find should be an "empty bottle".  #dip it into another
potion and you'll get an extra bottle of that potion (blessed or cursed as
the bottle was).  #dip it in a fountain to get water.  #dip it into a sink
to get water (usually) with a possibility of something else (same probability
as the sink has for drinking a non-water potion.

7) My pet idea (repeated): Improve the wand of probing by letting it identify
a pile of objects.

8) This next stuff should probably be #ifdef HOLIDAY - to strip for small 
machines: How about doubling leprechauns hit points on St Patrick's Day?
Seems fair.  Also, on Xmas (I am ashamed to admit I came into work on
Xmas and played Nethack this year) there should a Santa Claus '@' who
wanders up and gives the player a few presents (and maybe even a wish).
Once a game of course.  Obviously eggs should be Easter Eggs around
Easter (a little harder to calculate Easter though).  Needless to say,
undead creatures should be more fearsome on Halloween.  And of course
potions of booze should be more common on New Year's Eve --- Uh, forget
that last one. #endif

9) How about a notepad - not part of inventory, just a little something
to scribble on that is saved with the saved game, to jot notes to yourself
on where you have left things, what intrinisics you have, etc.  I have
seen suggestions where this would an inventory item - I contend it is for
player convenience.  Otherwise, I would keep a handwritten sheet if the
notepad cost me carrying weight or could be lost.

10) A cute minor touch on hallucinating:  make up some monster names that
would never be seen (e.g. yellow snake, two-headed baby) and occasionally
identify monsters that way.  Now we're talking hallucinations!

11) Engraving should take several turns.  This puts a premium on thoughtful
use of the "E" word in advance of trouble, rather than a bail-out.

12) I'll add my voice to the crowd that calls for treating sacks/chests 
more like your pack - i.e. move items in/out by letter.

13) I also agree with calls to limit the god's patience.  In particular,
a long time required between gifts, and refusal to heal after more than one
self-inflicted wound (until full HP reached).

14) Actions that hurt a player's luck should be flagged "you feel unlucky".
Only people who watch this group, or get spoilers in other fashion (such
as reading the source) are likely to be able to understand the consequences
of certain wrongheaded actions.  This is one issue where I stand firmly
with the "make it easier" crowd.

15) Since nymphs have no trouble removing cursed armor, etc. from a player,
when the player polymorphs into a nymph she should be able to remove her own
cursed armor and rings, and unwield a cursed weapon.

16) A somewhat difficult idea: Allowing the player to get an arbitrary Nethack
message clarified by the Oracle (for a handsome fee).  e.g. the player gets
"Your health is currently amplified" and doesn't know what that means. 
For a couple of thousand zorkmids, the Oracle explains that he/she now has
intrinsic shock resistance.  In discover mode, put an Oracle on level one,
and allow some number of freebies.  This is an excellent way to get a beginner
to tackle what seems to be an imposing game.

